1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,966
Kedarnath temple, buried under snow for 400 years?

2
00:00:04,966 --> 00:00:10,429
Some scientists say exactly that. And still the temple stood,

3
00:00:10,429 --> 00:00:11,919
straight and strong.

4
00:00:11,919 --> 00:00:14,327
But the story doesn't end here.

5
00:00:14,327 --> 00:00:18,339
In 2013, a flood of water and rocks came down from the mountain.

6
00:00:18,339 --> 00:00:22,352
The whole valley was destroyed, houses and shops were washed away.

7
00:00:22,352 --> 00:00:26,208
But the temple? A huge rock came and stopped right behind the temple.

8
00:00:26,208 --> 00:00:28,662
And the water split into two,

9
00:00:28,662 --> 00:00:31,116
and flowed out on both sides of the temple.

10
00:00:31,116 --> 00:00:33,938
People call this rock the Bhim Shila.

11
00:00:33,938 --> 00:00:37,112
They believe Bhim himself placed it there.

12
00:00:37,112 --> 00:00:39,581
What does science say about this?

13
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:43,273
In this video, there are not three, just these questions.

14
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:47,786
Who built the temple? Was it really buried under snow?

15
00:00:47,786 --> 00:00:50,248
And how did it survive in 2013?

16
00:00:50,248 --> 00:00:52,407
Every answer will come with proof,

17
00:00:52,407 --> 00:00:56,365
and every proof with its source. But let's start from the place

18
00:00:56,365 --> 00:00:59,244
where the story of Kedarnath begins.

19
00:00:59,244 --> 00:01:03,405
The war of Mahabharat was over. The Pandavas had won at Kurukshetra,

20
00:01:03,405 --> 00:01:07,944
but they had the blood of their own brothers and teachers on their hands.

21
00:01:07,944 --> 00:01:09,828
The folk tale of Garhwal says,

22
00:01:09,828 --> 00:01:12,466
this was considered the sin of gotra hatya.

23
00:01:12,466 --> 00:01:16,235
Gotra hatya means killing the people of your own family.

24
00:01:16,235 --> 00:01:19,530
Who could free them from this sin?

25
00:01:19,530 --> 00:01:22,001
The Pandavas got the answer: only Shiv.

26
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:26,120
And the search for Shiv led them towards the Himalayas.

27
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:27,758
But there is a twist in the story.

28
00:01:27,758 --> 00:01:31,033
Shiv did not want to meet the Pandavas at all. It is said

29
00:01:31,033 --> 00:01:34,309
he took the form of a bull and hid in the mountains.

30
00:01:34,309 --> 00:01:38,323
Bhim recognised the bull. He ran to catch it.

31
00:01:38,323 --> 00:01:40,732
But the bull started sinking into the ground,

32
00:01:40,732 --> 00:01:43,542
and only its hump was left above.

33
00:01:43,542 --> 00:01:44,642
Devotees believe

34
00:01:44,642 --> 00:01:47,942
that the triangular rock worshipped in Kedarnath

35
00:01:47,942 --> 00:01:49,409
is that very hump.

36
00:01:49,409 --> 00:01:53,076
That is why the shivling here looks different from other places.

37
00:01:53,076 --> 00:01:56,972
The folk tale says, then the Pandavas built the first temple here.

38
00:01:56,972 --> 00:01:59,700
After that they went towards Swargarohini,

39
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:03,986
that is, the mountain from where the path to heaven is believed to begin.

40
00:02:03,986 --> 00:02:07,619
This is a story, and no written proof of it has been found.

41
00:02:07,619 --> 00:02:10,162
But the real question starts now.

42
00:02:10,162 --> 00:02:12,705
The stone temple that stands today,

43
00:02:12,705 --> 00:02:14,885
what happened to it over the centuries?

44
00:02:14,885 --> 00:02:19,343
Right behind the temple there is a glacier, called Chorabari.

45
00:02:19,343 --> 00:02:22,464
A glacier means a huge river of ice,

46
00:02:22,464 --> 00:02:26,030
that moves very slowly, year after year.

47
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:26,745
Now think,

48
00:02:26,745 --> 00:02:31,037
did this glacier ever move forward and reach the temple?

49
00:02:31,037 --> 00:02:34,614
One scientist asked this very question, and set out to find the answer.

50
00:02:34,614 --> 00:02:35,719
Dr. R. K.

51
00:02:35,719 --> 00:02:39,401
Chaujar studied the rocks around Kedarnath.

52
00:02:39,401 --> 00:02:43,083
His paper was published in a science journal called Current Science.

53
00:02:43,083 --> 00:02:45,286
His method was very unusual.

54
00:02:45,286 --> 00:02:48,812
A thin layer grows on rocks,

55
00:02:48,812 --> 00:02:52,778
called lichen. Like moss, green-yellow in colour.

56
00:02:52,778 --> 00:02:56,461
Lichen grows very slowly, only a little each year.

57
00:02:56,461 --> 00:02:59,407
So by measuring its size, you can tell

58
00:02:59,407 --> 00:03:02,353
how long a rock has been lying out in the open.

59
00:03:02,353 --> 00:03:05,156
Where there was ice, lichen cannot grow.

60
00:03:05,156 --> 00:03:09,009
Only after the ice goes away does its clock start ticking.

61
00:03:09,009 --> 00:03:10,761
This was Chaujar's proof.

62
00:03:10,761 --> 00:03:11,973
He looked at moraines.

63
00:03:11,973 --> 00:03:17,226
Moraines are long heaps of rocks and soil carried along with a glacier,

64
00:03:17,226 --> 00:03:20,459
which get left behind when the ice melts.

65
00:03:20,459 --> 00:03:24,429
Now comes the period called the Little Ice Age.

66
00:03:24,429 --> 00:03:28,038
That is, a few centuries when the world's climate turned cold,

67
00:03:28,038 --> 00:03:29,482
and glaciers moved forward.

68
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:31,307
According to Chaujar's estimate,

69
00:03:31,307 --> 00:03:37,694
during this cold period the Kedarnath temple stayed buried under ice for about 400 years.

70
00:03:37,694 --> 00:03:41,800
Just think, such long cold, and the temple still stands!

71
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,763
But one paper is not enough.

72
00:03:43,763 --> 00:03:46,380
If the ice really passed over the temple,

73
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:49,979
there should be marks of it on the stones too. Were they found?

74
00:03:49,979 --> 00:03:55,778
After 2013, scientists from the Wadia Institute of Himalayan Geology in Dehradun came to Kedarnath.

75
00:03:55,778 --> 00:03:59,506
This institute studies the Himalayan mountains.

76
00:03:59,506 --> 00:04:02,986
They examined the outer walls of the temple closely.

77
00:04:02,986 --> 00:04:07,161
On the heavy granite stones they saw long yellowish streaks.

78
00:04:07,161 --> 00:04:10,674
These are called striations. That is, scratch marks,

79
00:04:10,674 --> 00:04:16,823
formed when ice and the gravel trapped in it rub against a rock.

80
00:04:16,823 --> 00:04:20,621
In some places the stone had also become smooth, like polished.

81
00:04:20,621 --> 00:04:23,383
As if someone had scrubbed it for centuries.

82
00:04:23,383 --> 00:04:25,454
This work is done by the sliding ice.

83
00:04:25,454 --> 00:04:27,717
According to scientists of the Wadia Institute,

84
00:04:27,717 --> 00:04:29,979
these marks were formed over many centuries.

85
00:04:29,979 --> 00:04:34,882
It means ice had touched these stones in one form or another.

86
00:04:34,882 --> 00:04:38,057
So the temple that withstood centuries of ice,

87
00:04:38,057 --> 00:04:41,232
could it have withstood one night of water?

88
00:04:41,232 --> 00:04:43,217
That very test came in 2013.

89
00:04:43,217 --> 00:04:47,898
It was the month of June. The pilgrimage was going on in Kedarnath,

90
00:04:47,898 --> 00:04:51,174
the valley was full of pilgrims and shops.

91
00:04:51,174 --> 00:04:53,983
Just then the sky opened up and started pouring.

92
00:04:53,983 --> 00:04:57,981
Near the Chorabari glacier, the Wadia Institute had a camp.

93
00:04:57,981 --> 00:05:02,380
A rain-measuring machine was installed there. What it recorded

94
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:03,579
was terrifying.

95
00:05:03,579 --> 00:05:08,017
Within just 24 hours, 325 millimetres of rain fell.

96
00:05:08,017 --> 00:05:10,236
This was not ordinary rain.

97
00:05:10,236 --> 00:05:13,343
This was a river falling from the sky.

98
00:05:13,343 --> 00:05:14,870
Where did all that water go?

99
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:17,925
On the mountain slopes the ice was melting,

100
00:05:17,925 --> 00:05:19,452
the streams were overflowing.

101
00:05:19,452 --> 00:05:22,507
And above, a lake was quietly filling up.

102
00:05:22,507 --> 00:05:25,743
This lake was called Chorabari Lake.

103
00:05:25,743 --> 00:05:30,366
Its wall was not built by any human. It was a moraine,

104
00:05:30,366 --> 00:05:33,602
that is, a heap of stones and soil.

105
00:05:33,602 --> 00:05:36,515
A wall of mud, and behind it, steadily rising water.

106
00:05:36,515 --> 00:05:40,155
Now the only question was, how long would this wall hold?

107
00:05:40,155 --> 00:05:44,524
No one knew the answer would come in just a few hours.

108
00:05:44,524 --> 00:05:48,041
The next morning. About 2 kilometres above the temple,

109
00:05:48,041 --> 00:05:51,558
the weak wall of Chorabari Lake suddenly broke.

110
00:05:51,558 --> 00:05:55,954
All the water that had collected rushed down at once.

111
00:05:55,954 --> 00:05:59,602
The Wadia Institute's watchmen later told what they saw.

112
00:05:59,602 --> 00:06:03,250
The entire lake emptied in less than 10 minutes.

113
00:06:03,250 --> 00:06:06,706
Just think, an entire lake in less than 10 minutes.

114
00:06:06,706 --> 00:06:10,929
And that water did not come alone. Big boulders,

115
00:06:10,929 --> 00:06:13,617
mud and trees were also being swept along.

116
00:06:13,617 --> 00:06:17,685
Below was the Kedarnath market, rest houses, and pilgrims.

117
00:06:17,685 --> 00:06:21,300
Nothing stood before the water and debris.

118
00:06:21,300 --> 00:06:24,916
So many families were separated forever.

119
00:06:24,916 --> 00:06:29,987
And this flood was heading straight along the path where the temple stood.

120
00:06:29,987 --> 00:06:34,636
The back of the temple, its north wall, was right in front.

121
00:06:34,636 --> 00:06:38,031
So something should have happened to the temple, right?

122
00:06:38,031 --> 00:06:41,427
But when the water receded, people were stunned.

123
00:06:41,427 --> 00:06:42,936
The temple was still standing.

124
00:06:42,936 --> 00:06:46,158
Behind the temple lay a huge rock.

125
00:06:46,158 --> 00:06:48,574
This rock wasn't there before.

126
00:06:48,574 --> 00:06:52,199
The flood itself carried it down from the mountain above.

127
00:06:52,199 --> 00:06:54,567
The Archaeological Survey of India, that is ASI,

128
00:06:54,567 --> 00:06:57,723
looks after the country's old monuments.

129
00:06:57,723 --> 00:07:02,064
According to them, this rock stopped about 9 metres behind the temple.

130
00:07:02,064 --> 00:07:06,672
9 metres, about the distance across a big room.

131
00:07:06,672 --> 00:07:10,769
A rock this big, in water this fast,

132
00:07:10,769 --> 00:07:13,329
stopping exactly there. How?

133
00:07:13,329 --> 00:07:15,087
Now see the real wonder.

134
00:07:15,087 --> 00:07:19,921
According to ASI, this rock acted like a shield.

135
00:07:19,921 --> 00:07:23,436
The water and debris did not hit the temple directly.

136
00:07:23,436 --> 00:07:26,446
The rock split the flood into two parts.

137
00:07:26,446 --> 00:07:29,791
One stream went past the left of the temple, the other past the right.

138
00:07:29,791 --> 00:07:31,463
The temple was left standing in the middle.

139
00:07:31,463 --> 00:07:35,020
Just like a rock in the middle of a river cuts the water,

140
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:37,607
it was the same. Only here the river was not of water,

141
00:07:37,607 --> 00:07:39,224
it was of stones and debris.

142
00:07:39,224 --> 00:07:42,974
The priests and devotees gave this rock a name, Bhim Shila.

143
00:07:42,974 --> 00:07:48,088
They believe Bhim placed it there to protect Shiv's abode.

144
00:07:48,088 --> 00:07:51,755
On one side, faith; on the other, the ASI report.

145
00:07:51,755 --> 00:07:55,422
Both talk about the same rock.

146
00:07:55,422 --> 00:08:00,464
But one more question remains. Was the rock alone enough?

147
00:08:00,464 --> 00:08:03,944
The water must have pushed hard on both sides of the temple.

148
00:08:03,944 --> 00:08:06,033
Still, why did the walls not fall?

149
00:08:06,033 --> 00:08:09,165
The answer is hidden in how the temple's stones were built.

150
00:08:09,165 --> 00:08:11,203
ASI's investigation found that

151
00:08:11,203 --> 00:08:13,580
no mortar was used in this temple.

152
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:17,996
Mortar means the cement or lime mix that binds stones together.

153
00:08:17,996 --> 00:08:21,479
Here the heavy stones were cut and placed one on top of another,

154
00:08:21,479 --> 00:08:23,569
fitted like a puzzle.

155
00:08:23,569 --> 00:08:26,007
Every stone is locked into the next.

156
00:08:26,007 --> 00:08:29,240
And the joints are held with a grip like iron strips,

157
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:32,474
called clamps. They don't let the stones slip.

158
00:08:32,474 --> 00:08:34,414
The stones move, but they don't fall apart.

159
00:08:34,414 --> 00:08:35,549
According to ASI,

160
00:08:35,549 --> 00:08:40,844
because of this very construction, the temple withstood earthquake tremors and the heavy pressure of water,

161
00:08:40,844 --> 00:08:44,247
It can handle both. The structure has flexibility, and strength too.

162
00:08:44,247 --> 00:08:45,382
And strength too.

163
00:08:45,382 --> 00:08:48,035
So the ice marks, the Bhim Shila,

164
00:08:48,035 --> 00:08:50,687
and the strong construction, it all looks clear.

165
00:08:50,687 --> 00:08:54,335
But here's the twist. The scientists themselves don't agree with each other.

166
00:08:54,335 --> 00:08:56,724
The first dispute is about the ice.

167
00:08:56,724 --> 00:09:00,706
Was the temple really buried inside a moving glacier?

168
00:09:00,706 --> 00:09:03,095
Or was the ice just piled up around it?

169
00:09:03,095 --> 00:09:07,117
One side says the glacier passed over the temple,

170
00:09:07,117 --> 00:09:09,933
which is why there are scratch marks on the walls.

171
00:09:09,933 --> 00:09:13,151
This is what the Wadia Institute team said.

172
00:09:13,151 --> 00:09:14,861
The other side says,

173
00:09:14,861 --> 00:09:19,562
maybe mounds of moraine just piled up all around the temple.

174
00:09:19,562 --> 00:09:23,836
And snow kept falling from above, covering it up to the roof.

175
00:09:23,836 --> 00:09:24,963
The most interesting part?

176
00:09:24,963 --> 00:09:28,719
Chaujar's own paper doesn't pick one answer either.

177
00:09:28,719 --> 00:09:32,851
It puts forward 3 different possibilities, and doesn't decide.

178
00:09:32,851 --> 00:09:36,700
Maybe the temple was built before the glacier came.

179
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:40,550
Or maybe the ice came and went back, and the temple was built after that.

180
00:09:40,550 --> 00:09:41,304
Or else,

181
00:09:41,304 --> 00:09:45,452
the temple was somehow built in the middle of the glacier or along its edge.

182
00:09:45,452 --> 00:09:46,583
The paper says,

183
00:09:46,583 --> 00:09:49,977
right now no one can say for sure.

184
00:09:49,977 --> 00:09:52,588
The second dispute is even bigger.

185
00:09:52,588 --> 00:09:55,635
Who built the stone temple we see today?

186
00:09:55,635 --> 00:09:58,682
One side says Adi Shankaracharya,

187
00:09:58,682 --> 00:10:01,293
the other says Raja Bhoj.

188
00:10:01,293 --> 00:10:03,669
There's a gap of centuries between the two,

189
00:10:03,669 --> 00:10:06,385
and neither side has solid written proof.

190
00:10:06,385 --> 00:10:09,779
So even the real age of the temple is still a mystery.

191
00:10:09,779 --> 00:10:10,953
And the Bhim Shila?

192
00:10:10,953 --> 00:10:16,043
In such a fast flood, why did that rock stop right behind the temple?

193
00:10:16,043 --> 00:10:19,566
Scientists still haven't been able to fully explain it.

194
00:10:19,566 --> 00:10:22,303
Did the slope of the land stop it?

195
00:10:22,303 --> 00:10:25,432
Or did it hit another rock and get stuck?

196
00:10:25,432 --> 00:10:28,560
Every answer is a guess, not solid proof.

197
00:10:28,560 --> 00:10:31,525
So the thing that seemed the most certain

198
00:10:31,525 --> 00:10:34,067
became the biggest question.

199
00:10:34,067 --> 00:10:38,727
But you can see all this for yourself with your own eyes.

200
00:10:38,727 --> 00:10:42,196
Kedarnath still stands today, above the Mandakini valley,

201
00:10:42,196 --> 00:10:44,123
Among the snow-covered mountains.

202
00:10:44,123 --> 00:10:48,363
During the pilgrimage season, long lines of devotees form here.

203
00:10:48,363 --> 00:10:52,403
Go behind the temple. The Bhim Shila is still lying there today.

204
00:10:52,403 --> 00:10:55,709
People apply tilak on it, fold their hands,

205
00:10:55,709 --> 00:10:57,178
and take photos.

206
00:10:57,178 --> 00:11:00,357
Look carefully at the outer walls of the temple.

207
00:11:00,357 --> 00:11:02,830
Look for yellow lines on the granite stones.

208
00:11:02,830 --> 00:11:06,009
These are believed to be old marks left by the snow.

209
00:11:06,009 --> 00:11:09,768
Look at the joints between the stones too. You won't find cement anywhere,

210
00:11:09,768 --> 00:11:11,439
just tightly fitted stones.

211
00:11:11,439 --> 00:11:15,198
This is the construction that stood up to the flood.

212
00:11:15,198 --> 00:11:18,029
And look up toward the Chorabari glacier.

213
00:11:18,029 --> 00:11:20,861
The very place where it all began,

214
00:11:20,861 --> 00:11:24,501
and where the snow once came down this far.

215
00:11:24,501 --> 00:11:26,179
So, back to the same question.

216
00:11:26,179 --> 00:11:30,376
Did the Kedarnath temple stay buried under snow for 400 years?

217
00:11:30,376 --> 00:11:32,474
Some evidence says yes,

218
00:11:32,474 --> 00:11:35,411
some scientists are still thinking about it.

219
00:11:35,411 --> 00:11:40,379
And how did it survive in 2013? One rock, a strong structure,

220
00:11:40,379 --> 00:11:43,541
and Bhim's blessing for the devotees.

221
00:11:43,541 --> 00:11:46,702
Everyone has their own answer.

222
00:11:46,702 --> 00:11:50,154
In the next video, we will move on to another mystery,

223
00:11:50,154 --> 00:11:52,071
Dwarka, sunk under the sea.

224
00:11:52,071 --> 00:11:55,523
There too, evidence and legend stand face to face.

225
00:11:55,523 --> 00:12:00,904
Now a question for you. Have you ever been to Kedarnath,

226
00:12:00,904 --> 00:12:05,687
and seen the Bhim Shila? Tell us in the comments,

227
00:12:05,687 --> 00:12:11,666
and send this to a friend who is about to go to Kedarnath.
